From 59ecb831dbc6a87f1a71bd8fd278c6e1b242dd42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Hooper <ryanh@openssl.foundation>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 11:40:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] DTLS 1.3: Accept an ACK while the server awaits the client's
 flight

After sending its first flight, a DTLS 1.3 server waits in
TLS_ST_EARLY_DATA for the client's Certificate or Finished. A client
that has received only part of that flight may ACK what it did receive
(RFC 9147 section 7.1), and the server is then meant to retransmit only
the unacknowledged part (section 7.2). The read transition did not
accept an ACK in this state, so the server aborted the handshake with
an unexpected_message alert.

Accept DTLS13_MT_ACK in TLS_ST_EARLY_DATA and process it through
TLS_ST_SR_ACK, as is already done for the KeyUpdate, ticket and
post-handshake authentication states. It is not accepted while a
HelloRetryRequest is pending, where only the second ClientHello is
valid.

A client may also ACK the whole flight before it has sent Finished.
dtls_process_ack() would then finish the read, and write_transition()
would move to TLS_ST_OK without the client's Finished ever being read.
Keep reading in that case, as is already done when an ACK interrupts a
post-handshake authentication response.

Add two tests. In the first, the server's first flight is split over
several datagrams, the middle one is lost, and the client ACKs the
ServerHello and the Finished through its own record layer. The server
must accept the ACK, retire exactly those two messages, retransmit
only the others, and complete the handshake. In the second, the client
has received the whole flight but is held back from replying while it
produces its client certificate, and ACKs every record. The server must
keep waiting for the client's flight instead of completing the
handshake.

Fixes: #33242
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-sonnet-5-5
Reviewed-by: Tomas Mraz <tomas@openssl.foundation>
Reviewed-by: Mounir Idrassi <mounir.idrassi@idrix.fr>
